# Perspective and Full-Footprint Grounding

Apply this rule whenever creating, editing, generating, scaling, positioning, cropping, or animating image-based objects.

## Construct the scene

1. Define the camera before placing objects: perspective type, horizon/eye level, ground level, vanishing point(s), viewing distance, and viewing angle.
2. Draw or overlay the perspective grid and explicit floor, shelf, desk, or other support plane. Lock these guides while composing.
3. Establish a scale key using one known reference object. Objects at equal depth must share scale; objects moving in depth must scale along the grid.
4. Preserve intrinsic aspect ratio unless intentional distortion is documented. Inspect transparent padding and meaningful opaque bounds.

## Ground the entire base

5. Define each object's projected footprint—not merely its lowest visible pixel. Mark every required contact point: all feet, legs, wheels, base corners, or the complete bottom plane.
6. Project every contact marker onto the same support plane. A chair requires every intended leg to meet the floor; a desk requires its whole base; a laptop or book requires its complete supporting edge or plane.
7. The support polygon must contain the object's complete footprint. No part may overhang, float, or sink unless intentionally designed.
8. Do not accept edge-only contact. If one corner touches but another required contact point is above or below the projected plane, the object is floating or sinking.
9. Follow the plane's perspective; do not force all contacts onto one horizontal screen line when the support plane recedes toward a vanishing point.
10. Add a restrained contact shadow attached across the footprint, consistent with the scene light. A shadow supports grounding but never replaces geometric contact.

## Preserve spatial relationships

11. Project parallel edges toward the established vanishing point(s), and keep furniture depth, thickness, and proportions consistent with grid cells.
12. Resolve occlusion from footprints and depth: nearer objects may cover farther objects, but supports must remain visually understandable.
13. When an object continues into another scene, retain its identity, proportions, perspective, support relationship, and surrounding context.

## Verify before completion

14. Review screenshots with temporary overlays showing horizon, vanishing lines, support planes, footprint polygons, and contact markers.
15. Measure all critical contact markers against the projected support plane, accounting for transparent pixels; verify polygon containment rather than one `getBoundingClientRect()` edge.
16. Check desktop, 390 × 844 mobile, a short landscape viewport, and transition start/midpoint/end. Reject any view where one required contact marker separates from its support.
17. Remove debug guides only after checks pass. Do not publish while any object floats, sinks, changes scale incorrectly, or has unexplained overlap.

